<!--
    A call loses its arguments when the caller forwards three or more of its
    own argument registers untouched and loads a later one itself.

        int getopt_long(int argc, char **argv, const char *opts, void *lo, int *ix)
        { return internal(argc, argv, opts, lo, ix, 0, 0); }   /* 7 parameters */
        int w5(int a, char **b, const char *c, void *d)
        { return in5(a, b, c, d, 0) + 1; }                       /* 5 parameters */
        int get_int(const void *blob, int node, const char *prop, int def)
        { int len; const unsigned *cell = getprop(blob, node, prop, &amp;len); ... }

    clang 22 -O2 (-fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector -fcf-protection=none,
    linked -nostdlib -static -Ttext=0x401000, every callee defined in the
    image).  Each caller hands rdi, rsi, rdx (and rcx, r8) on untouched and
    writes only the last register (r8d = 0, r9d = 0, rcx = &amp;len), plus
    getopt_long's seventh argument at [rsp].  kuna printed `internal();`,
    `in5() + 1` and `getprop()`: passthrough leaves the trials of forwarded
    registers inactive until it extends the final list, and
    `forceInactiveChain` read three or more of them in a row as the end of
    the argument list, so the register the caller loaded after them went too
    and left the list with nothing to extend.  getopt_long's callee reads all
    six argument registers, a run passthrough did not take as an arity claim.

    Stage: P4 active input-trial scoring (kuna_passthrough.rs, option
    passthrough, on by default).  A forwarded register the callee takes fills
    its slot in the chain rule; a callee body that reads the whole register
    file is taken at a call whose caller also stores the first stack
    argument.  Pass 1 is the default; pass 2 sets passthrough off, where all
    three calls print with no arguments.  The ARM shapes the fill must not
    reach are in kuna-passthroughslot-arm.xml.

    Two gcc -O2 shapes at 0x402000 the fill must not reach either.  g3 is
    `h3(a,b,c) + a` with IPA-RA keeping a in ecx across the call (`mov
    %edi,%ecx; call h3; add %ecx,%eax`); h3 (`imul %esi,%edi; lea
    (%rdi,%rdx),%eax; ret`) never reads rcx, so ecx ends the list after the
    three forwarded registers, not h3(a0,a1,a2,a0).  pid_plus is
    `sys1(39) + x` after a prologue `push %rbx`; sys1 is a syscall() body that
    reads every argument register, and a pushed callee-saved register is no
    stack argument, so its run stays no arity claim, not
    sys1(0x27,a1,a2,a3,a4,a5).
-->
